course: dictionary
lesson: 10223
title: Toadstools and mushrooms
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A toadstool is a fungus with a round cap on a stalk, a bit like a mushroom. Fungi are not plants or animals. The toadstool is the part you see above the ground when the fungus sends up its fruit. «The red toadstool with white spots, the fly agaric, is poisonous.» The word poisonous tells you that some toadstools can make you very ill.
explain: The word toadstool is often used for the kinds that are poisonous or not eaten. Look at the next part of the sentence: «the death cap, which looks plain and harmless, can kill». Harmless means it cannot hurt you, but here the sentence shows that looks can be deceiving. A plain-looking toadstool can still be deadly.
explain: Read the end of the sentence: «nobody should ever eat a wild mushroom unless an expert has checked it». The word unless means there is one exception: an expert has checked it. So the safe rule is simple. Do not pick or eat wild ones.
fact: A toadstool is a fungus with a round cap on a stalk, like a mushroom.
fact: The fly agaric is a red toadstool with white spots, and it is poisonous.
fact: The death cap looks plain and harmless, but it can kill.
fact: Toadstools grow in damp woods, especially in autumn, and help rot down dead leaves and wood.
fact: Never pick or eat wild mushrooms or toadstools, and wash your hands after touching them.
exercise: «The red toadstool with white spots, the fly agaric, is poisonous.» What is the fly agaric? | A tree in a damp wood | A blue flower in a garden | *A red toadstool with white spots | A kind of mushroom you can eat safely
exercise: Which toadstool looks plain and harmless but can kill? | The fly agaric | *The death cap | A mushroom bought in a shop | A blackberry bush
exercise: Where do toadstools often grow, especially in autumn? | In a hot, dry desert | *In damp woods | On the top of a tall mountain | Inside a glass jar on a shelf
test: What should you do with a wild toadstool you find in a wood? | *Leave it alone and never eat it | Take it home to cook | Taste a tiny piece | Pick it to show a friend
test: Why does the text say nobody should eat a wild mushroom unless an expert has checked it? | It is a rule from a fairy tale | Because they all taste bad | *Because some look harmless but are deadly | Because they are too big to eat
exam: In fairy tales, what do fairies, gnomes and pixies often sit on? | Golden clouds | Wooden benches | Mossy stones | *Red-and-white toadstools
